Pacman
======

A personal, non-commercial tribute to Namco's 1980 arcade game.
Pac-Man is a trademark of Bandai Namco; this program is not
connected with them.

You guide Pac-Man round a maze, eating every dot while the ghosts
chase you. This first version has the maze, the dots, the energizers
and four ghosts who chase you and head for their corners by turns;
energizers turn the ghosts blue and you can eat them (200 to 1600,
and 12000 for four sweeps); there is bonus fruit (70 and 170 dots
eaten) and each level ends when the last dot goes, with the dots
back for the next. You have three lives (one extra at 10000);
when the last is lost, GAME OVER shows. Blinky speeds up when few
dots are left (Cruise Elroy), and everyone gets faster on later
levels. There is synthesised sound (a start tune, waka, a siren,
and more), and a title with the ghosts introduced, a pause menu, a
Settings screen and a How to play page. The ten best scores are
kept, and a game in the top ten asks for your name (see High
scores below).

Starting
--------
Double-click !Pacman. It takes over the whole screen (or opens in a
window, if you chose that last time) and shows the title: Play, High
scores, Settings, How to play, View source and Desktop. Its icon
stays on the icon bar, where MENU gives Info, Full screen, Window,
Browser full screen, Sound (ticked when on), View source, Open
directory and Quit.

Keys
----
  Arrow keys or W A S D          steer Pac-Man (so do ' / Z X, up,
                                 down, left, right, and a gamepad)
  Return or Space                choose a menu item (left and
                                 right change a setting)
  P                              pause, and again to go on
  Escape                         in a game, the pause menu (Continue,
                                 Restart, Settings, Title, Desktop);
                                 on the title, back to the desktop
  F                              browser full screen on and off

Press a turn early: Pac-Man can start it up to three pixels before
the middle of a corner, or finish it up to three after, cutting the
corner. The ghosts are slowed in the tunnel; Pac-Man is not.

The demo
--------
Leave the title alone for ten seconds and a silent demo game plays
itself (DEMO shows in the maze). It ends at the first lost life or
after a minute, the high scores show for six seconds and the title
returns. Any key or click during it goes back to the title and
does nothing else. Nothing it scores is recorded.

High scores
-----------
Choose High scores on the title for the table: rank, name, score,
level and date. After GAME OVER a score in the top ten asks for a
name of up to three characters (letters, digits, space, . and -;
Backspace deletes; Return or Escape finishes; empty gives ???). The
table is kept in Choices:Pacman.Scores; delete that file to go back
to the starting table. HIGH SCORE on the screen is the best in the
table.

Settings
--------
Choose Settings on the title or the pause menu: Display (full screen
or window), Browser (in page or full screen), Sound, Volume (20 to
100%), Lives (1, 2, 3 or 5) and Bonus life (10000, 15000, 20000 or
none). They are remembered in Choices:Pacman.Settings. Sound
starts at the first click or key and is also scaled by the
desktop's own volume.

Reading and changing it
-----------------------
The whole game is JavaScript in this directory. Choose View source
on the icon bar menu, or Shift-double-click !Pacman and then a file,
to read it in !JsEdit. It is built on GameLib, in
<GameLib$Dir>, whose own !Help lists its parts.

The guide is $.Docs.Pacman.
